  "step_2": "The individual considered the probabilities and outcomes of both lotteries. Specifically, they noted that Lottery A offers 2400 Euros with 33% probability and Lottery B offers 2500 Euros with 34% probability. They observed that the difference in probability is only 1% (34% - 33%), while the difference in the prize is 100 Euros (2500 - 2400), which they interpreted as a relatively larger difference (approximating it as 'more than 1%' or 'about 5%'). The individual justified their choice of Lottery B by stating that they chose it because they believed the probability difference was 'vernachlässigbar' (negligible), and thus preferred the higher prize despite the slightly lower probability. The individual explicitly acknowledged the trade-off between probability and payoff and emphasized the smallness of the probability difference relative to the payoff difference.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason prefers the lottery with the higher probability of a favorable outcome. In this case, Lottery B has a higher probability (34% vs. 33%), so the reason would favor B. The individual also chose Lottery B. In their justification, they explicitly recognized that the probability difference was small ('nur 1% Differenz') and considered it negligible, which aligns with the idea that the probability difference is not decisive. However, their actual justification was not based solely on preferring the higher probability — instead, they prioritized the higher payoff (2500 vs. 2400) because the probability difference was deemed insignificant. This suggests that the individual did not use the reason as a primary decision rule, but rather used it as a secondary consideration (i.e., the probability difference was small enough to ignore). The decision reason would have led to choosing B due to higher probability, but the individual’s reasoning was driven more by the payoff advantage when probability differences were seen as negligible. Therefore, while the reason is consistent with the outcome, it is not the core driver of the decision. The individual used a different, more payoff-weighted reasoning process. Thus, the decision reason was not the primary basis for the choice.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
}

